Both No}\cell\pard\plain \s187\loch\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
Nothing changes for either}\cell\row\pard\pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\nowidctlpar\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
Now think from one prisoner's point of view}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
Ask: "No matter what my opponent does, is it better for me to say Yes or No?"}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af9 \ltrch\hich\af3\loch\f3 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls11 \li1418\ri0\lin1418\rin0\fi-283\sl276\slmult1\tx0\li709\ri0\lin709\rin0\fi-283\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
If opponent says Yes:}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
 Saying Yes gets you freedom (both leave). Saying No }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs43\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\b0\loch
also}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
 gets you freedom (you're the "no" guy who leaves). So in this case it doesn't matter \u8212\'97 you leave either way. }
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af9 \ltrch\hich\af3\loch\f3 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls11 \li1418\ri0\lin1418\rin0\fi-283\sl276\slmult1\tx0\li709\ri0\lin709\rin0\fi-283\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
If opponent says No:}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
 Saying Yes means your sentence doubles (worst possible outcome for you). Saying No means nothing changes \u8212\'97 bad, but far better than doubling. }
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
So compare the two choices:}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af9 \ltrch\hich\af3\loch\f3 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls12 \li1418\ri0\lin1418\rin0\fi-283\sl276\slmult1\tx0\li709\ri0\lin709\rin0\fi-283\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
Say }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
Yes}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
: best case = freedom, worst case = doubled sentence }
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af9 \ltrch\hich\af3\loch\f3 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls12 \li1418\ri0\lin1418\rin0\fi-283\sl276\slmult1\tx0\li709\ri0\lin709\rin0\fi-283\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
Say }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
No}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
: best case = freedom, worst case = no change (status quo) }
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
No}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
 is never worse than }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
Yes}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
, and in one scenario it's much better. This makes "No" the }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
dominant strategy}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
 \u8212\'97 the rational choice regardless of what the other person does.}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
The paradox}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
If both prisoners think this way (and they will, if they're rational and self-interested), they'll }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
both say No}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
. Result: nothing changes, both stay in prison.}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
But notice \u8212\'97 the }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs43\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\b0\loch
best joint outcome}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
 was actually }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
both say Yes}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
, where both go free! The individually "safe" choice for each person, when both make it, leads to a worse result than if they'd both trusted each other.}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa0\ltrpar{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
This is the essence of the Prisoner's Dilemma: }{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\cs15\rtlch\ab \ltrch\loch\b\b0\loch
individually rational decisions can produce a collectively irrational outcome}{\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch
. It shows up everywhere beyond literal prisoners \u8212\'97 arms races, price wars between companies, climate agreements, tragedy-of-the-commons situations. Trust and cooperation would benefit everyone, but without a way to guarantee the other side cooperates too, the "safe" self-interested move wins out, and everyone ends up worse off than they could have been.}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar{\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\loch\fs22\b0\loch
If it were a repeated game (they play this many times, not just once), the dynamics change \u8212\'97 strategies like "tit-for-tat" (cooperate first, then mirror what the other did last time) tend to outperform pure selfishness over the long run, because trust can be built and punished for betrayal. Want me to go into that?}
\par \pard\plain \s183\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\afs32\ab \ltrch\loch\fs32\b\loch
T}{\rtlch\afs30\ab \ltrch\loch\fs30\b\loch
wo camels were flying: one red, the other to the left. How much does a kilogram of asphalt weigh if the hedgehog is four years old?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
That's a fun bit of absurdist logic \u8212\'97 like something out of Soviet-era joke culture (the camel/hedgehog nonsense riddles). It's intentionally nonsensical: camels don't fly, "the other to the left" isn't a color, and the hedgehog's age has nothing to do with asphalt's weight.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
But if we play it straight on the one real, answerable part hidden in there: a kilogram of asphalt weighs exactly 1 kilogram \u8212\'97 that's what "kilogram" means, regardless of hedgehogs, camels, or flight paths. \uc0 \u55357\uc1 \u56836\'3f}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar{\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Want me to make up an equally absurd riddle back, or were you testing how I handle nonsense logic?}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\afs32\ab \ltrch\loch\fs32\b\loch
2-4-16-64,whats next}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
Looking at the pattern: 2, 4, 16, 64 \u8212\'97 each term is the previous term to a specific power relationship, or more simply: it's powers of 4.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
4\u185\'3f = 4}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
4\u178\'3f = 16}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
4\u179\'3f = 64}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\ql\ltrpar{\loch
4\u8308\'3f = 256}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar{\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\loch\fs22\b0\loch
So the next number is 256 (each term after 2 is multiplied by 4).}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar\rtlch\ab0 \ltrch\loch\b0\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af11\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1058\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1058\kerning1\dbch\af12\langfe2052\loch\sl276\slmult1\qc\tx0\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\ltrpar{\rtlch\afs52\ab \ltrch\loch\fs52\b\loch
